1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,710
Why does your cat knead you like a lump of dough?

2
00:00:02,710 --> 00:00:05,443
Left paw, right paw, purring, eyes half shut.

3
00:00:05,443 --> 00:00:08,021
Why would a grown predator do that at all?

4
00:00:08,621 --> 00:00:10,818
We'll talk more about that in this video.

5
00:00:12,021 --> 00:00:14,288
You finally drop onto the couch.

6
00:00:14,288 --> 00:00:16,914
Long day, sore feet, remote in hand.

7
00:00:16,914 --> 00:00:19,040
And before you've even found something to watch,

8
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:21,546
there's a weight on your lap. The cat.

9
00:00:21,546 --> 00:00:24,095
It turns around once. Then twice.

10
00:00:24,095 --> 00:00:25,832
Then it gets to work.

11
00:00:25,832 --> 00:00:28,414
Left paw, right paw. Left paw, right paw.

12
00:00:28,414 --> 00:00:30,723
Slow, steady pushes into your thighs,

13
00:00:30,723 --> 00:00:32,799
like a tiny baker who's got a lot of dough

14
00:00:32,799 --> 00:00:34,390
to get through before morning.

15
00:00:34,390 --> 00:00:36,313
The eyes go half shut.

16
00:00:36,313 --> 00:00:39,768
The purring kicks in, loud as a little outboard motor.

17
00:00:39,768 --> 00:00:41,389
And then, every so often,

18
00:00:41,389 --> 00:00:43,539
a claw goes right through your jeans

19
00:00:43,539 --> 00:00:45,029
and you say something you wouldn't say

20
00:00:45,029 --> 00:00:46,569
in front of your grandmother.

21
00:00:46,569 --> 00:00:48,440
Kneading, people call it.

22
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:51,850
Online it's got a much better name. Making biscuits.

23
00:00:51,850 --> 00:00:52,937
If you live with a cat,

24
00:00:52,937 --> 00:00:55,061
you've almost certainly been the biscuit.

25
00:00:55,061 --> 00:00:57,447
But have you ever stopped to wonder why?

26
00:00:57,447 --> 00:00:59,317
Why would a grown animal, a predator,

27
00:00:59,317 --> 00:01:00,854
built to stalk and pounce,

28
00:01:00,854 --> 00:01:03,789
climb onto a person and start kneading them like bread?

29
00:01:03,789 --> 00:01:06,186
It goes back a long way. All the way back

30
00:01:06,186 --> 00:01:08,606
to the first day of a cat's life, actually.

31
00:01:08,606 --> 00:01:10,928
And the answer isn't one neat thing.

32
00:01:10,928 --> 00:01:12,869
It's a baby habit, a comfort thing,

33
00:01:12,869 --> 00:01:13,829
a kind of signature,

34
00:01:13,829 --> 00:01:15,803
and maybe a leftover from the wild too,

35
00:01:15,803 --> 00:01:19,002
all tangled up together. So let's untangle it.

36
00:01:19,002 --> 00:01:20,775
First, the first day.

37
00:01:20,775 --> 00:01:23,616
A kitten comes into the world blind and deaf.

38
00:01:23,616 --> 00:01:25,034
Its eyes stay sealed shut

39
00:01:25,034 --> 00:01:27,170
for roughly the first week or two.

40
00:01:27,170 --> 00:01:30,303
It can't walk properly. It can't keep itself warm.

41
00:01:30,303 --> 00:01:32,206
Honestly, it can't do much of anything,

42
00:01:32,206 --> 00:01:34,168
except find its mother and eat.

43
00:01:34,168 --> 00:01:37,232
And that's where kneading starts. When a newborn kitten nurses,

44
00:01:37,232 --> 00:01:39,767
it pushes against its mother's belly with its front paws,

45
00:01:39,767 --> 00:01:42,664
one after the other. Push, push, push.

46
00:01:42,664 --> 00:01:45,989
That pressure on the milk glands helps get the milk flowing.

47
00:01:45,989 --> 00:01:48,487
More pushing, more milk. Simple deal.

48
00:01:48,487 --> 00:01:51,190
Kittens start doing it in their very first days,

49
00:01:51,190 --> 00:01:53,974
long before they can even see what they're pushing on.

50
00:01:53,974 --> 00:01:55,988
Nobody shows them how.

51
00:01:55,988 --> 00:01:58,679
It's just there, wired in from the start.

52
00:01:58,679 --> 00:02:00,088
So picture what kneading means

53
00:02:00,088 --> 00:02:02,335
to a kitten in those first few weeks.

54
00:02:02,335 --> 00:02:05,113
It means warmth. Food. A big purring mother.

55
00:02:05,113 --> 00:02:08,256
A heap of brothers and sisters all squashed together.

56
00:02:08,256 --> 00:02:09,604
Pretty much everything good

57
00:02:09,604 --> 00:02:12,926
in its whole little world happens while those paws are going.

58
00:02:12,926 --> 00:02:14,114
Then the kitten grows up,

59
00:02:14,114 --> 00:02:16,335
the way kittens do, which is fast.

60
00:02:16,335 --> 00:02:19,149
Somewhere around eight weeks or so, it's weaned.

61
00:02:19,149 --> 00:02:22,485
Solid food now. It's off exploring, wrestling its siblings,

62
00:02:22,485 --> 00:02:24,546
ambushing its first toy mouse.

63
00:02:24,546 --> 00:02:26,471
The milk is long gone.

64
00:02:26,471 --> 00:02:28,563
The kneading, though? That stays.

65
00:02:28,563 --> 00:02:31,492
And that's the first big reason your cat kneads you.

66
00:02:31,492 --> 00:02:34,504
It's kitten behavior that never got switched off.

67
00:02:34,504 --> 00:02:36,356
A surprising amount of what house cats

68
00:02:36,356 --> 00:02:40,137
do as grownups is really baby stuff that just stuck around.

69
00:02:40,137 --> 00:02:41,391
They meow at us,

70
00:02:41,391 --> 00:02:44,553
even though adult cats hardly ever meow at each other.

71
00:02:44,553 --> 00:02:47,289
They trot up to us with their tails held straight up,

72
00:02:47,289 --> 00:02:49,317
the way a kitten greets its mother.

73
00:02:49,317 --> 00:02:50,752
And they knead.

74
00:02:50,752 --> 00:02:53,310
Think about when your cat actually does it.

75
00:02:53,310 --> 00:02:56,337
Usually it's relaxed. Sprawled on a soft blanket,

76
00:02:56,337 --> 00:02:58,969
settled on a warm lap, drifting toward a nap.

77
00:02:58,969 --> 00:03:01,258
Its whole body is saying, this feels like being a kitten

78
00:03:01,258 --> 00:03:03,658
again. Safe. Warm. Fed.

79
00:03:03,658 --> 00:03:05,797
And the paws just go back to the thing they used

80
00:03:05,797 --> 00:03:08,361
to do when life felt exactly like this.

81
00:03:08,361 --> 00:03:12,149
Look closely and you'll often catch other kitten signs tagging along.

82
00:03:12,149 --> 00:03:14,672
The purring, for one. Kittens purr while they nurse,

83
00:03:14,672 --> 00:03:16,698
and the mother purrs right back at them.

84
00:03:16,698 --> 00:03:19,285
Some cats drool while they knead, which is a bit gross,

85
00:03:19,285 --> 00:03:21,073
I'll admit, and a bit baffling

86
00:03:21,073 --> 00:03:24,105
until you remember what kneading used to bring. A drooling,

87
00:03:24,105 --> 00:03:26,826
kneading cat is, more or less, remembering milk.

88
00:03:26,826 --> 00:03:28,647
And some cats take it one step further

89
00:03:28,647 --> 00:03:30,790
and suck on a blanket, or your sleeve,

90
00:03:30,790 --> 00:03:33,492
or a soft toy, kneading away the whole time.

91
00:03:33,492 --> 00:03:35,714
Nursing on fleece, basically.

92
00:03:35,714 --> 00:03:38,840
So when your cat climbs aboard and starts making biscuits,

93
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:40,350
there's a real sense in which it's

94
00:03:40,350 --> 00:03:42,638
treating you a little bit like its mom.

95
00:03:42,638 --> 00:03:45,194
You're warm, you're soft, you're safe, and, crucially,

96
00:03:45,194 --> 00:03:47,130
you're the one who fills the food bowl.

97
00:03:47,130 --> 00:03:49,214
From where the cat's sitting, which is on you,

98
00:03:49,214 --> 00:03:51,938
that's a pretty good job description for a mother.

99
00:03:51,938 --> 00:03:54,327
Comfort isn't the whole story though.

100
00:03:54,327 --> 00:03:57,277
There's something else going on under those paws.

101
00:03:57,277 --> 00:03:59,898
Cats have scent glands all over the place.

102
00:03:59,898 --> 00:04:01,950
On their cheeks. Their chin.

103
00:04:01,950 --> 00:04:03,854
The base of the tail. And on their feet,

104
00:04:03,854 --> 00:04:06,060
in the pads and in between the toes.

105
00:04:06,060 --> 00:04:07,678
Every step a cat takes leaves

106
00:04:07,678 --> 00:04:09,988
a faint trace of its smell behind it.

107
00:04:09,988 --> 00:04:13,008
You'll never notice. Another cat definitely will.

108
00:04:13,008 --> 00:04:15,841
It's part of why cats scratch things, by the way.

109
00:04:15,841 --> 00:04:17,467
When your cat drags its claws down

110
00:04:17,467 --> 00:04:19,793
the side of the sofa you paid good money for,

111
00:04:19,793 --> 00:04:22,628
yes, it's sharpening, and yes, it's stretching.

112
00:04:22,628 --> 00:04:24,936
But it's also leaving a scent mark.

113
00:04:24,936 --> 00:04:26,520
And a visible one.

114
00:04:26,520 --> 00:04:29,502
Right there, at eye level, for everybody to see.

115
00:04:29,502 --> 00:04:32,349
Kneading does the same job, only softer.

116
00:04:32,349 --> 00:04:34,648
Each push of the paw presses a little of that scent

117
00:04:34,648 --> 00:04:38,015
into whatever's underneath. The blanket. The pillow.

118
00:04:38,015 --> 00:04:39,613
Your lap. So when your cat kneads you,

119
00:04:39,613 --> 00:04:42,791
it might also be quietly labeling you. Mine.

120
00:04:42,791 --> 00:04:44,523
This one is mine.

121
00:04:44,523 --> 00:04:46,644
Bit possessive? Sure. Very.

122
00:04:46,644 --> 00:04:48,139
But from a cat's point of view

123
00:04:48,139 --> 00:04:50,099
it's also kind of a compliment.

124
00:04:50,099 --> 00:04:51,834
Cats only bother spreading their scent

125
00:04:51,834 --> 00:04:54,543
on things they feel good about. Their bed.

126
00:04:54,543 --> 00:04:57,303
Their favorite sunny spot. Their people.

127
00:04:57,303 --> 00:04:59,990
You won't catch them marking the vacuum cleaner.

128
00:04:59,990 --> 00:05:01,525
Now, there's one more theory,

129
00:05:01,525 --> 00:05:04,152
and this one goes all the way back to the wild.

130
00:05:04,152 --> 00:05:06,054
Before sofas, before fleece throws,

131
00:05:06,054 --> 00:05:08,756
there was long grass and heaps of dry leaves.

132
00:05:08,756 --> 00:05:12,218
Wild cats, including the wildcats our house cats came from,

133
00:05:12,218 --> 00:05:15,102
had to sleep wherever they could find a decent spot.

134
00:05:15,102 --> 00:05:17,140
And a patch of long grass isn't a bed

135
00:05:17,140 --> 00:05:18,950
until somebody flattens it.

136
00:05:18,950 --> 00:05:21,141
So the idea goes like this.

137
00:05:21,141 --> 00:05:22,556
A wild cat would trample

138
00:05:22,556 --> 00:05:25,020
and pat down its spot before lying in it.

139
00:05:25,020 --> 00:05:26,817
Press the grass flat.

140
00:05:26,817 --> 00:05:28,637
Make sure there's nothing sharp in there,

141
00:05:28,637 --> 00:05:31,618
nothing alive, nothing nasty hiding underneath.

142
00:05:31,618 --> 00:05:33,528
Then curl up. If that's right,

143
00:05:33,528 --> 00:05:35,802
then when your cat kneads the duvet before bed,

144
00:05:35,802 --> 00:05:38,076
turning in a circle and pushing the covers down,

145
00:05:38,076 --> 00:05:40,384
it's doing the exact same old job.

146
00:05:40,384 --> 00:05:42,512
Making the bed. Even though, let's be honest,

147
00:05:42,512 --> 00:05:44,156
the bed was already made.

148
00:05:44,156 --> 00:05:46,007
You made it. This morning.

149
00:05:46,007 --> 00:05:48,840
To be fair, nobody's proven this one. It's tricky

150
00:05:48,840 --> 00:05:51,934
to test what wild cats were thinking thousands of years ago,

151
00:05:51,934 --> 00:05:54,387
and they're not around to ask. But it does fit

152
00:05:54,387 --> 00:05:57,378
with how often cats knead right before they lie down.

153
00:05:57,378 --> 00:06:00,016
And dogs do something similar, don't they?

154
00:06:00,016 --> 00:06:04,041
That whole spinning-in-circles routine before they finally flop over.

155
00:06:04,041 --> 00:06:06,607
Kneading might also just feel nice.

156
00:06:06,607 --> 00:06:08,794
Watch a cat wake up from a nap.

157
00:06:08,794 --> 00:06:11,317
Out go the front legs, long and stiff.

158
00:06:11,317 --> 00:06:13,639
The toes spread. The claws flex.

159
00:06:13,639 --> 00:06:16,823
Kneading works those same muscles. Push, release.

160
00:06:16,823 --> 00:06:18,419
Push, release. A slow,

161
00:06:18,419 --> 00:06:21,406
rhythmic stretch for the shoulders, the legs, the paws.

162
00:06:21,406 --> 00:06:23,182
If you've ever had a really good stretch

163
00:06:23,182 --> 00:06:24,890
in bed first thing in the morning,

164
00:06:24,890 --> 00:06:26,600
you already know the feeling.

165
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:29,641
And then there's the calming side. Rhythm is soothing.

166
00:06:29,641 --> 00:06:31,652
It is for us, anyway.

167
00:06:31,652 --> 00:06:33,203
Think of rocking in a chair,

168
00:06:33,203 --> 00:06:34,871
or jiggling your foot under the desk

169
00:06:34,871 --> 00:06:36,508
when you're waiting on something.

170
00:06:36,508 --> 00:06:38,740
It seems to work for cats too.

171
00:06:38,740 --> 00:06:41,232
Plenty of cats knead when they're happy.

172
00:06:41,232 --> 00:06:43,698
But some knead when they're a little rattled.

173
00:06:43,698 --> 00:06:46,099
After a move. After a trip to the vet.

174
00:06:46,099 --> 00:06:48,906
When the house is loud and full of strangers.

175
00:06:48,906 --> 00:06:50,605
It's a way to settle.

176
00:06:50,605 --> 00:06:53,477
The cat is, in its own way, comforting itself.

177
00:06:53,477 --> 00:06:56,783
Female cats sometimes knead more at certain times, too.

178
00:06:56,783 --> 00:06:59,110
A cat in heat often gets extra cuddly,

179
00:06:59,110 --> 00:07:01,937
rolling around on the floor, rubbing on everything in sight,

180
00:07:01,937 --> 00:07:04,602
and yes, kneading. And a mother cat getting close

181
00:07:04,602 --> 00:07:07,358
to giving birth will often knead and paw at blankets,

182
00:07:07,358 --> 00:07:08,358
fussing over them,

183
00:07:08,358 --> 00:07:10,742
getting a nest ready for the kittens on the way.

184
00:07:10,742 --> 00:07:13,789
So no, not every cat kneads for the same reason.

185
00:07:13,789 --> 00:07:15,897
And the same cat might knead for one reason

186
00:07:15,897 --> 00:07:19,025
on Tuesday and a totally different one on Friday.

187
00:07:19,025 --> 00:07:21,693
Here's something else that surprises people.

188
00:07:21,693 --> 00:07:23,846
Not every cat does it at all.

189
00:07:23,846 --> 00:07:27,144
Some cats knead every single evening, like clockwork.

190
00:07:27,144 --> 00:07:29,291
Some do it once in a blue moon.

191
00:07:29,291 --> 00:07:30,883
And some never seem to do it,

192
00:07:30,883 --> 00:07:32,642
at least not where you can see.

193
00:07:32,642 --> 00:07:36,143
That's completely normal. A cat that doesn't knead isn't unhappy,

194
00:07:36,143 --> 00:07:37,981
and it doesn't love you any less.

195
00:07:37,981 --> 00:07:40,538
It just isn't its thing. And the styles!

196
00:07:40,538 --> 00:07:42,374
They're all over the place.

197
00:07:42,374 --> 00:07:44,848
Some cats only use the front paws.

198
00:07:44,848 --> 00:07:46,744
Some throw the back paws in as well

199
00:07:46,744 --> 00:07:48,622
and sort of march on the spot.

200
00:07:48,622 --> 00:07:50,403
Some knead slow and dreamy,

201
00:07:50,403 --> 00:07:52,522
some like they're running late for something.

202
00:07:52,522 --> 00:07:55,126
Some keep their claws tucked away politely.

203
00:07:55,126 --> 00:07:57,875
Others go full claws, every single one.

204
00:07:57,875 --> 00:08:00,076
And then there are the cats that knead nothing at all,

205
00:08:00,076 --> 00:08:03,469
just paws pumping away in empty air, eyes half closed,

206
00:08:03,469 --> 00:08:05,253
off somewhere in their own head.

207
00:08:05,253 --> 00:08:07,460
People call that making air biscuits,

208
00:08:07,460 --> 00:08:10,432
which might be the best phrase on the entire internet.

209
00:08:10,432 --> 00:08:12,906
It isn't only a house cat thing, either.

210
00:08:12,906 --> 00:08:15,376
Big cats do it too. Lions, tigers

211
00:08:15,376 --> 00:08:18,296
and other big cats have been filmed kneading blankets and bedding,

212
00:08:18,296 --> 00:08:19,931
purring away like a very large,

213
00:08:19,931 --> 00:08:21,230
very heavy version of the one

214
00:08:21,230 --> 00:08:23,299
on your couch. Okay.

215
00:08:23,299 --> 00:08:25,997
Let's talk about the part that actually hurts.

216
00:08:25,997 --> 00:08:28,381
The claws. When a cat pushes down with its paw,

217
00:08:28,381 --> 00:08:31,215
it spreads its toes. And when it spreads its toes,

218
00:08:31,215 --> 00:08:33,662
the claws tend to slide right out with them.

219
00:08:33,662 --> 00:08:35,837
So a blissed-out cat kneading your lap can turn

220
00:08:35,837 --> 00:08:37,435
into a little set of needles going

221
00:08:37,435 --> 00:08:38,917
in and out of your legs,

222
00:08:38,917 --> 00:08:40,441
one paw at a time.

223
00:08:40,441 --> 00:08:42,588
It's not doing it on purpose.

224
00:08:42,588 --> 00:08:44,425
It's not attacking you.

225
00:08:44,425 --> 00:08:47,209
In that moment it's so relaxed it isn't really keeping

226
00:08:47,209 --> 00:08:49,225
track of what its claws are up to.

227
00:08:49,225 --> 00:08:51,137
So what do you do about it?

228
00:08:51,137 --> 00:08:53,166
Well, first, don't punish it.

229
00:08:53,166 --> 00:08:55,535
Don't yell, and don't shove the cat off.

230
00:08:55,535 --> 00:08:56,502
From the cat's side,

231
00:08:56,502 --> 00:08:58,246
it was showing you about as much trust

232
00:08:58,246 --> 00:09:00,973
and affection as a cat knows how to show.

233
00:09:00,973 --> 00:09:02,059
Tell it off for that,

234
00:09:02,059 --> 00:09:04,456
and it may decide your lap isn't such a safe place

235
00:09:04,456 --> 00:09:05,516
after all.

236
00:09:05,516 --> 00:09:07,935
Which is the last thing you want, really.

237
00:09:07,935 --> 00:09:10,028
There are much easier fixes.

238
00:09:10,028 --> 00:09:12,421
Keep the claws trimmed. Just the sharp tips,

239
00:09:12,421 --> 00:09:14,021
carefully, and if you're not sure how,

240
00:09:14,021 --> 00:09:15,571
ask your vet to show you.

241
00:09:15,571 --> 00:09:18,432
Throw a thick blanket or a folded towel over your lap

242
00:09:18,432 --> 00:09:20,169
before the cat climbs on.

243
00:09:20,169 --> 00:09:22,090
Then it can knead to its heart's content

244
00:09:22,090 --> 00:09:24,433
and the claws hit fabric instead of you.

245
00:09:24,433 --> 00:09:26,363
You can also gently move its paws,

246
00:09:26,363 --> 00:09:28,000
or shift the cat over a bit,

247
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:30,496
so it's kneading the blanket beside you instead.

248
00:09:30,496 --> 00:09:32,881
And some people find that stroking the cat's back

249
00:09:32,881 --> 00:09:34,362
while it kneads calms it down enough

250
00:09:34,362 --> 00:09:37,004
that it settles and lies down sooner.

251
00:09:37,004 --> 00:09:39,447
One thing you should never do is have a cat declawed

252
00:09:39,447 --> 00:09:42,967
to stop it. Declawing isn't some heavy-duty nail trim.

253
00:09:42,967 --> 00:09:45,570
It removes the last bone of each toe.

254
00:09:45,570 --> 00:09:47,373
It's banned in a lot of countries,

255
00:09:47,373 --> 00:09:50,354
and vets and cat welfare groups widely oppose it.

256
00:09:50,354 --> 00:09:52,794
Kneading is normal cat behavior.

257
00:09:52,794 --> 00:09:55,534
The answer is a blanket. Not surgery.

258
00:09:55,534 --> 00:09:58,258
Is kneading ever something to worry about?

259
00:09:58,258 --> 00:09:59,841
Mostly, no.

260
00:09:59,841 --> 00:10:02,675
It's about as normal as cat behavior gets.

261
00:10:02,675 --> 00:10:03,863
But there are a couple of things

262
00:10:03,863 --> 00:10:06,708
worth keeping an eye on. Sucking is one.

263
00:10:06,708 --> 00:10:08,854
A cat that sucks on a blanket now and then

264
00:10:08,854 --> 00:10:10,976
while it kneads is generally fine.

265
00:10:10,976 --> 00:10:13,088
Some cats go further, though.

266
00:10:13,088 --> 00:10:15,144
They suck and chew on wool, on fabric,

267
00:10:15,144 --> 00:10:18,493
even on plastic, and sometimes they swallow bits of it.

268
00:10:18,493 --> 00:10:20,458
That's sometimes called wool sucking,

269
00:10:20,458 --> 00:10:22,231
and it shows up more in certain breeds,

270
00:10:22,231 --> 00:10:24,129
like Siamese and Burmese.

271
00:10:24,129 --> 00:10:26,327
In some cats it's also thought to be linked

272
00:10:26,327 --> 00:10:28,881
to being taken away from their mother too early.

273
00:10:28,881 --> 00:10:30,816
If your cat is actually eating fabric,

274
00:10:30,816 --> 00:10:31,927
have a chat with your vet,

275
00:10:31,927 --> 00:10:34,271
because swallowed cloth can block the gut.

276
00:10:34,271 --> 00:10:36,261
The other thing is change.

277
00:10:36,261 --> 00:10:37,627
If a cat that's always kneaded

278
00:10:37,627 --> 00:10:39,804
a little suddenly starts kneading all the time,

279
00:10:39,804 --> 00:10:43,306
obsessively, or alongside other changes, like hiding,

280
00:10:43,306 --> 00:10:45,199
crying, or going off its food,

281
00:10:45,199 --> 00:10:47,228
that can mean something's bothering it.

282
00:10:47,228 --> 00:10:49,702
Stress, maybe. Or something physical.

283
00:10:49,702 --> 00:10:52,206
Any time a cat's behavior shifts out of nowhere,

284
00:10:52,206 --> 00:10:54,236
a vet check is a smart idea.

285
00:10:54,236 --> 00:10:56,097
Most of the time, though?

286
00:10:56,097 --> 00:10:58,397
A kneading cat is a happy cat.

287
00:10:58,397 --> 00:10:59,366
Or at the very least,

288
00:10:59,366 --> 00:11:01,679
an extremely comfortable one. So.

289
00:11:01,679 --> 00:11:03,646
Why do cats knead you?

290
00:11:03,646 --> 00:11:06,055
Because it's the very first thing they ever learned,

291
00:11:06,055 --> 00:11:07,424
on their very first day,

292
00:11:07,424 --> 00:11:09,958
pressing into their mother for milk and warmth.

293
00:11:09,958 --> 00:11:11,661
Because that feeling of being small

294
00:11:11,661 --> 00:11:13,863
and safe never quite leaves them.

295
00:11:13,863 --> 00:11:15,950
Because their paws carry their scent,

296
00:11:15,950 --> 00:11:18,420
and you're something they'd like to mark as theirs.

297
00:11:18,420 --> 00:11:19,557
Because it might be an old

298
00:11:19,557 --> 00:11:21,588
wild habit of making a bed.

299
00:11:21,588 --> 00:11:23,540
Because it's a good stretch.

300
00:11:23,540 --> 00:11:26,372
And because, quite simply, it calms them down.

301
00:11:26,372 --> 00:11:29,139
But mostly, I think, because you feel safe.

302
00:11:29,139 --> 00:11:32,042
A cat doesn't make biscuits for just anyone.

303
00:11:32,042 --> 00:11:34,291
It saves that for the warm, soft places

304
00:11:34,291 --> 00:11:36,608
where it can let its guard all the way down.

305
00:11:36,608 --> 00:11:38,638
So the next time the cat climbs onto your lap,

306
00:11:38,638 --> 00:11:41,571
turns around twice, and starts that slow left paw,

307
00:11:41,571 --> 00:11:43,658
right paw, maybe grab a blanket.

308
00:11:43,658 --> 00:11:46,156
Maybe trim those claws this weekend.

309
00:11:46,156 --> 00:11:48,650
And take it as the compliment it is.

310
00:11:48,650 --> 00:11:50,294
That's a grown predator telling you,

311
00:11:50,294 --> 00:11:52,076
in the oldest language it knows,

312
00:11:52,076 --> 00:11:53,623
that you feel like home.
